Working tree|The working tree contains the checked-out files you edit.|It can differ from both the index and the current commit. Git status separates tracked modifications, staged changes and untracked files. Inspect these states before switching branches or running operations that replace local content.|git status --short
Staging|The index, or staging area, records the exact content intended for the next commit.|git add copies selected content into the index; later working-tree edits are not automatically staged. Review the staged diff before committing. This allows a coherent commit without including every local change.|git diff --staged
Commits|A commit records a tree snapshot, parent relationships and metadata.|Commits form a directed history rather than storing only textual patches. A commit's identity changes if its content or metadata changes. Keep commits focused enough that review, rollback and bisection remain useful.|git show --stat HEAD
Branches|A branch is a movable reference to a commit.|New commits advance the current branch while other branch references remain where they were. Branches do not provide separate remote permissions by themselves. Worktrees can provide separate checked-out directories for concurrent local work.|git branch -vv
Fetch|Fetch downloads objects and updates remote-tracking references without merging them into the current branch.|It lets you inspect upstream changes before choosing merge, rebase or another integration strategy. Remote-tracking references describe the last fetched state, not guaranteed real-time remote state.|git fetch origin
Merge|Merge integrates histories and may create a commit with multiple parents.|Git finds a common ancestor and combines changes, requiring human resolution when changes conflict. A clean textual merge can still be semantically wrong. Run relevant tests and inspect the combined behavior.|git log --graph --oneline --all -20
Ignore rules|Ignore rules keep matching untracked files out of routine Git status and add operations.|They do not remove files already tracked or erase secrets from history. Use them for generated output and local configuration, while handling credential exposure through revocation and an appropriate cleanup process.|git check-ignore -v PATH
Diffs|A diff compares selected snapshots or file states.|Plain git diff compares working tree with index; --staged compares index with HEAD. Commit ranges and merge-base comparisons answer different review questions. Choose the comparison explicitly to avoid overlooking staged or upstream changes.|git diff --check
Rebase|Rebase recreates commits on a different base to change history shape.|New commit identities result, and conflicts may need resolution per replayed commit. Avoid rewriting shared history without coordination. Test the final tree because conflict resolutions can alter behavior even when the history looks cleaner.|git log --oneline --decorate -10
Revert|Revert creates a new commit that applies the inverse of an earlier change.|It preserves published history and is often suitable for shared branches. Later changes and merge commits complicate reversal, so review the resulting diff and run tests. Reverting application code does not reverse external database or infrastructure effects.|
Reset|Reset moves a reference and, depending on mode, changes the index and working tree.|Soft reset preserves index and files; mixed reset updates the index; hard reset also replaces tracked working-tree content. Understand which states contain valuable work before choosing a mode. Reflog recovery does not guarantee recovery of uncommitted file content.|
Cherry-pick|Cherry-pick applies the change introduced by a selected commit onto the current branch.|It creates a new commit identity and may conflict if context differs. Confirm prerequisite changes exist, especially for backports. Copying a single fix without its dependency can compile incorrectly or change behavior unexpectedly.|
Conflict resolution|A conflict requires a deliberate choice about how competing changes should combine.|Read both sides and surrounding intent, resolve the file, stage it and continue the interrupted operation. Removing markers is only a syntactic step. Verify the resulting behavior and ensure no required change was lost.|git status
Reflog|The reflog records recent movements of local references.|It can help locate commits after reset, rebase or accidental branch movement. Entries are local and expire under retention and garbage-collection rules. Find the intended commit and preserve it with a new reference before further cleanup.|git reflog --date=iso -10
Bisect|Bisection narrows a regression by testing commits between known-good and known-bad points.|A deterministic test lets Git choose intermediate commits efficiently. Skip revisions that cannot be tested for unrelated reasons and restore the original checkout afterward. Flaky tests can point bisection at the wrong change.|
Force with lease|A force push with a lease updates rewritten history only if the remote reference matches an expected value.|An explicit expected commit protects against overwriting an unexpected collaborator update. It remains a history rewrite and requires coordination. Inspect the remote head after a rejected lease instead of retrying blindly.|
Continuous integration|CI runs automated checks when changes are proposed or integrated.|Fast, reliable checks catch integration problems near the change. Required checks should validate the actual revision intended for merge. A passing pipeline is limited by its coverage and environment; it is not proof of every production behavior.|
Artifacts|A build artifact is a versioned output produced by a build and consumed by testing or deployment.|Build once and promote the same immutable artifact where possible. Record its source revision and digest so deployments are traceable. Retention and access policy matter because artifacts can contain proprietary code or sensitive data.|
CI secrets|CI secrets provide credentials to authorized jobs without storing them in source.|Workflow trust boundaries determine which code can read them. Untrusted pull-request code, logs and uploaded artifacts can leak secrets if privileges are mixed. Prefer short-lived scoped credentials and avoid granting deployment access to arbitrary test code.|
Dependency pinning|Dependency pinning constrains the versions or identities used by a build.|Lock files and immutable action or image references improve reproducibility. A pinned vulnerable dependency still needs updates. Review dependency changes and maintain an intentional refresh process rather than choosing between unrestricted updates and permanent freezing.|
Caching|CI caches reuse intermediate data to reduce build time.|Keys should include inputs that determine validity, such as lock files and platform. Cache contents are an optimization, not a trusted release artifact. Prevent untrusted jobs from poisoning caches consumed by privileged release jobs.|
Deployment gates|Deployment gates require defined checks or decisions before promotion.|Examples include tests, artifact verification, environment approval and health checks. Bind the gate to the actual artifact and destination. An approval for one revision should not silently authorize a different artifact built afterward.|
Rollback|Rollback returns a deployment to a known compatible version or configuration.|It requires an available artifact and compatibility with current data and dependencies. Schema or irreversible side effects may require forward recovery instead. Test rollback procedures and measure health after restoration rather than stopping at a successful deployment command.|
Concurrency controls|Concurrency controls prevent conflicting jobs from changing shared deployment state simultaneously.|A deployment lock or queue can serialize updates to one environment while allowing independent work elsewhere. Cancelling a running job may leave partial changes, so define cleanup and recovery. A branch name alone is not a sufficient environment lock.|
OIDC federation|OIDC federation lets a CI workload exchange a signed identity token for short-lived service credentials.|The receiving trust policy should constrain repository, workflow context and environment as appropriate. Audience and subject validation prevent unintended callers. Short-lived credentials reduce persistence risk but do not make an overprivileged job safe.|
Monorepo checks|Monorepo checks select validation for several projects stored in one repository.|Dependency-aware selection is more reliable than checking only directly changed files. Shared libraries, build configuration and tooling can affect many packages. Preserve a full validation path for changes whose impact is uncertain.|
Release tags|A release tag names a chosen commit as a versioned release point.|Annotated or signed tags can add metadata and verification, but policies must define trust and immutability. Match the tag, source commit and released artifact digest. Moving a published tag undermines reproducibility for consumers.|git tag --list
Flaky tests|A flaky test produces inconsistent outcomes without a relevant product change.|Timing, shared state and environmental assumptions are common causes. Track and fix the cause; unlimited retries can hide real regressions. Quarantine needs ownership and a deadline so missing coverage does not become permanent.|
Branch protection|Branch protection and rulesets constrain changes to important branches.|Required reviews, status checks and restricted updates support a controlled integration process. Configure bypass rights deliberately and ensure check names refer to trusted workflows. Repository rules complement, rather than replace, application testing.|
Supply-chain evidence|Supply-chain evidence links source, build process and released artifacts.|Provenance, signatures and dependency inventories support verification and investigation. Verify identity and digest at the deployment boundary. Evidence is only useful when the builder and signing permissions are themselves controlled.|
